A||||12||Gokart||Strange sound....why?||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||02-16-2000||02:38 PM||||When I turn my key I wait the usual 3 to 5 seconds for the fuel pump to do it's thing before turning her over. Now it's doing it AFTER I shut it off as well for about 10 to  15 seconds! This seems to have started after I put the plenum back on after repainting it, but I don't see why this would matter unless it's not the fuel pump I'm hearing. Anyone else here something going after they turn the ignition off? TIA for replys.||||reg||5
Z||000001||william_thorniii||02-16-2000||02:42 PM||||Gokart,<P>I believe that the pump is supposed to run after the ignition is killed.  Otherwise for those that crank before the pump runs, they would have to wait to build up pressure.  I could be wrong on this though.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000002||cobra427||02-16-2000||03:21 PM||||GoK, have always noticed that same thing.Never seemed to be a problem. Would agree with Will3||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||02-16-2000||03:36 PM||||I never notice the fuel pump running after the ignition is shut off. Mine runs 10 seconds or so if the overnight temps get around -15F or so. But only if I turn the ignition to "on", then "off" without ever cranking the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||02-16-2000||04:01 PM||||56,<P>I will experiment with mine right after work and let you all know tomorrow what happens with my 87 Duke.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000005||Rodrv6||02-16-2000||04:25 PM||||The fuel pump will run after you shut off the engine until the oil pressure bleeds off. The amount of time depends on the temperature of the oil, age of the engine and such.  A low mileage engine on a cold day that is not warmed up to operating temperature may hold oil pressure long enough to run the pump for 10 seconds or more. An older engine warmed up and on a warm day may only hold pressure for 1 or 2 seconds.  The pump is wired through the oil pressure switch as a back up in case the pump relay fails to operate.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gokart||02-16-2000||07:18 PM||||God I love this place! Thanks guys. The old me would usually hear something like that and immeadiatly assume somethings wrong. It's nice to know some noises are ok.||||reg||14
Z||000007||Wrench||02-16-2000||07:26 PM||||That is a good noise... believe me I know because mine stopped making that "noise" a few weeks ago. Talk about poor performance!! Pump replaced and that "great noise" is back.||||reg||12
Z||000008||JetroGT||02-17-2000||04:52 AM||||If for some reason the pump does not shut off, you will need to replace your oil sending unit. When it goes bad it will do the jumpy oil pressure gauge thing and sometimes make your fuel pump run constantly.||||reg||1
Z||000009||stsmithgt||02-18-2000||03:54 PM||||I had a pump make a similar noise that your talking about, BUT it was a lot louder than it should have been.  Plus my car would surge on the freeway.  Towards the end of the pumps life, it started messing with my fuel guage too.  It turned out to be an after market pump the previous owner put in.  The new one is much quieter (factory replacement) and doesn't make my fuel guage misread the fuel level.  Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
